course: biology
lesson: 295
title: Animal myths put right: elephants and mice, lemmings, ostriches and goldfish
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Animal myths are like a story passed along a line of children in a game of whispers: something true is seen, someone tells it a bit wrongly, and a cartoon or a film makes the wrong version famous.
explain: In Dumbo the grown-up elephants scream and climb onto a stool when a tiny mouse runs past, and in the old Lemmings computer game little creatures march off cliffs in a line. Both are fun, and both are wrong about real animals.
explain: Each myth usually hides something true: an ostrich lying flat with its neck on the ground looks headless from far away, and lemmings really do drown sometimes when huge crowds of them cross rivers.
explain: The way to put a myth right is the scientist's way: ask what the animal really does, test it fairly, and look at who first told the story and why.
fact: Elephants are not especially scared of mice; in tests they were startled by anything moving suddenly near their feet, and they are wary of bees, which can sting inside the trunk, so farmers in Africa build beehive fences to keep elephants off crops.
fact: Lemmings do not kill themselves; in years with very many lemmings they move to new places and some drown crossing water, and a 1958 Disney film faked its famous cliff scene by pushing lemmings over.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in the sand; they lie flat with their necks along the ground to hide, and they turn their eggs in the nest with their beaks, so from far away the head seems buried.
fact: Goldfish can remember things for months; they have been trained to push levers and swim through hoops, and pond fish learn their feeding times.
fact: No bat is blind; fruit bats see well, and many small bats also find insects in the dark by listening to the echoes of their own calls, which is called echolocation.
fact: Most birds have a poor sense of smell and will not reject a chick that a person touched, but it is still best to leave wild babies alone.
fact: Porcupines cannot shoot their quills; the quills come off easily when touched and stick into the attacker, and a porcupine rattles its quills and stamps its feet as a warning first.
fact: Bulls cannot see red well; it is the movement of the matador's cape that makes them charge, and a waving towel of any colour would do the same.
fact: In Britain the crane fly is called daddy long legs; it is not a spider and has no venom, and the cellar spiders also called daddy long legs have weak venom that is harmless to people.
fact: A duck's quack does echo; scientists tested it in a special sound room, and the echo is just hard to hear because the quack fades away softly.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to show their mood, to signal to other chameleons and to warm up or cool down; on a chessboard a chameleon would not turn checked.
fact: Hedgehogs eat beetles, worms and slugs, not apples; old pictures showed them carrying fruit on their spines, and milk makes hedgehogs ill, while cat food and water are safe for them.
exercise: Is it true that elephants are terrified of mice? | No, they are startled by any sudden movement near their feet | Yes, a mouse can climb right up the trunk and choke them to death | Yes, elephants faint at the sight of any mouse | Yes, mice nibble elephants' toenails at night
exercise: Why do beehive fences keep elephants away from crops? | Elephants avoid bees, which can sting inside the trunk | Elephants love honey and stay by the hives eating it all night | The fences give the elephants a small electric shock | Bees carry away the crops before elephants arrive
exercise: What really happens to lemmings in years when there are very many of them? | They move to new places and some drown crossing water | They all jump off cliffs together on purpose to make more room | They hide underground and sleep for a year | They turn white and fly away south
exercise: How did a 1958 Disney film get its famous lemming cliff scene? | The film makers pushed the lemmings over themselves | They filmed real wild lemmings choosing to jump into the sea | They drew the whole scene as a cartoon | They used toy lemmings made of rubber
exercise: What does an ostrich really do to hide? | It lies flat with its neck along the ground | It pushes its head deep down into the sand and waits | It climbs into the nearest thorn bush to hide | It digs a deep burrow in the sand and jumps in
exercise: How long can a goldfish remember something it learned? | For months | For three seconds | For about one minute | For one day only
exercise: Which is true about bats and their eyes? | All bats can see, and fruit bats see well | All bats are completely blind from the day they are born | Bats have no eyes at all, only very big ears | Only baby bats can see, and they lose it as they grow
exercise: How do many small bats find insects in the dark? | They listen to echoes of their own calls | They follow the light of the moon to the insects | They smell the insects from miles and miles away | They feel the wind the insects make with wings
exercise: Will a mother bird abandon a chick because a person touched it? | No, most birds have a poor sense of smell | Yes, she smells the person on it and leaves for good | Yes, but only if the person wore perfume | Only songbirds, never other birds
exercise: What makes a bull charge at the matador's cape? | The way the cape moves | The bright red colour of it | The smell of the cloth itself | The music of the brass band
exercise: What does a porcupine do when an animal comes too close? | It rattles its quills and stamps, and loose quills stick into an attacker | It fires its quills through the air at the attacker like a shower of arrows | It rolls into a ball and shoots spikes | It hides its quills and plays dead
exercise: What is the crane fly that people in Britain call daddy long legs? | An insect with no venom, not a spider | The most venomous spider in the whole world | A spider whose fangs are too small to bite | A baby harvestman that grows into a spider
exercise: What did scientists find when they tested a duck's quack in a sound room? | The quack does echo, but the echo is hard to hear | The quack never makes any echo at all | Only a mother duck's quack echoes | The quack echoes only under water
exercise: Why does a chameleon mostly change colour? | To show its mood, signal and warm up or cool down | To match any background it sits on | To copy the colours of the animal next to it | To show what it ate last
exercise: What does a hedgehog really eat? | Beetles, worms and slugs | Apples it carries on its spines | Mostly bread and milk | Grass and fallen leaves
exercise: What is safe to put out for a hedgehog in your garden? | Cat food and water | A saucer of cow's milk | Bread soaked in milk | A big pile of sweet apples
test: Count them: in the cartoon two elephants climb onto one stool to get away from a mouse. How many trunks and how many legs are on the stool? | 2 trunks and 8 legs | 2 trunks and 4 legs | 4 trunks and 8 legs | 1 trunk and 8 legs
test: «played that old Lemmings game, they just walk off the cliff lol, real ones do that right» What is right? | No, real lemmings do not jump on purpose; some drown when crowds cross water | Yes, they all jump every spring to make room for the young ones being born | Yes, but only the old ones jump | No, real lemmings cannot walk at all
test: «my nan says ostriches stick their heads in sand when scared» What would happen to a bird that really did that? | It could not see danger or breathe well, so it would not survive long | It would be perfectly safe, because lions cannot see a bird without a head | It would grow a longer neck | It would learn to breathe sand
test: In Finding Nemo the fish Dory forgets everything in seconds, and someone says goldfish are just like that. How could you test a goldfish's memory fairly? | Feed it in one corner after a signal for days, then give only the signal | Ask the goldfish a question out loud and wait to see if it swims to the answer | Show it a picture of a cat | Put it in a smaller bowl
test: «blind as a bat, innit» What is the best answer? | Wrong saying: all bats can see, and many also use echoes | Right, bats bump into walls and trees all day and all night | Right, bats are born with no eyes | Wrong, bats see only in daylight
test: «found a baby bird on the path and i touched it, now its mum'll dump it, i'm so stupid» What is true? | Most birds will not reject it because of your smell, but leave it alone now | Yes, the mother will smell you and never come back to that chick again | It must be washed in soap at once | You must now raise it in your bedroom
test: Why do people keep telling the myth that a bird will reject a touched chick? | It keeps children from picking up wild babies, which is a good thing | Because birds can smell far better than dogs, and every bird checks its chicks | Because scientists proved it long ago | Because it is true for every bird
test: A scientist waves a still red cape and a flapping blue towel at a bull. Which will it most likely charge? | The flapping blue towel | The still red cape | Neither, bulls only charge people | Both at once
test: «mate says daddy long legs is the deadliest spider, it just can't bite you» What is right? | No; the crane fly is not a spider, and the cellar spider's venom is harmless to us | Yes, its fangs are just too short to bite, but its venom is the strongest of all spiders | Yes, one drop could kill a horse | No, it is a tiny bird with long legs
test: In a cartoon a chameleon sits on a chessboard and turns checked on a chessboard. What would a real chameleon do? | Change colour mostly with its mood and warmth, not copy the squares | Turn into perfect black and white squares to match the board exactly | Turn see-through like glass | Turn the colour of the player
test: «hedgehog came in the garden, giving him a bowl of milk like in the book» What is best? | Give water and cat food; milk makes hedgehogs ill | Milk is the best food for hedgehogs, just like the book says | Give it a whole apple to carry off on its spines | Give it nothing, hedgehogs eat only leaves
test: Count them: 3 hedgehogs and 2 bats come into the garden at dusk. How many ears are there altogether? | 10 | 6 | 5 | 20
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reaction of elephants to small rodents. | Elephants are startled by any abrupt movement near their feet, not by mice as such | Elephants exhibit an innate and specific terror of mice, which may enter the trunk and obstruct it | Elephants are incapable of perceiving animals of small size | Elephants deliberately trample every rodent they encounter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the depiction of lemmings in a cinematographic production of 1958 is regarded as unreliable. | The animals were propelled over the cliff by the makers of the film | The animals were filmed in a region where lemmings do not occur | The animals depicted were in fact squirrels | The scene was recorded entirely at night
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vision of bats? | All bats possess sight, and numerous species additionally employ echolocation | Bats are universally devoid of sight and depend upon echolocation alone in all circumstances | Bats perceive solely by means of smell | Bats possess sight exclusively during daylight hours
exam: Ascertain the principal function of colour change in the chameleon. | The communication of mood and signals, and the regulation of temperature | The precise reproduction of any background whatsoever | The concealment of the animal from its own reflection | The attraction of insects by means of bright patterns
